# Copy this file to .env. Every setting has a working default except the model's API key and
# the database, which this file names for development.
# This file is for development on one machine. A deployment sets ENVIRONMENT=production and
# passes its settings through the process environment or a secret manager: the app then never
# copies this file into its environment, so the model keys below must arrive as real
# environment variables. In production the app also refuses to start with development's
# conveniences, and says which: the compose database's password, LOCAL_ACCOUNT=true, no
# STORAGE_URL, no APP_URL, no SMTP_URL, no SCANNER_URL, no HOSTING_PROVIDER, no OPERATOR_NAME,
# OPERATOR_EMAIL, BACKUP_DAYS or SERVER_LOG_DAYS, no AGENT_MODEL or INDEX_MODEL, or one its
# provider moves, a google: model without GOOGLE_PAID_TIER, no key for a model or its fallback,
# with DATA_REGION a piece outside the region, or a database, scanner, embeddings endpoint,
# model or bucket that would be reached unencrypted from outside the deployment's own network;
# and it serves no /docs. A staging deployment is production too.
# ENVIRONMENT=development
# How the API, the worker and the migrations log, to standard error: json, one object a line
# with the time in UTC, the level, the logger, the message and the ids of what it was logged
# about (the request's id, the user, organisation and project, or the job and its document or
# answer), for a collector to search; text, lines for a person at a terminal. Unset, json in
# production and text in development. The maintenance commands write text either way. There is
# no level to set: below INFO, the libraries would log the documents and questions they send.
# LOG_FORMAT=
# Where the API and the worker report the errors nobody planned for: a Sentry DSN from the
# project's settings ("Client Keys"), which GlitchTip and a Sentry of your own take too. Empty
# reports nothing; the log has every error either way. A report holds what the log would show:
# the error's kind and where in the code it happened, its message only when the app wrote it,
# and the ids of the request or job, never a document's text, a question, an answer, a key or
# an email address. The tracker is on the subprocessor list unless it runs on the deployment's
# own network; Sentry's EU region is a DSN under ingest.de.sentry.io. SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT names
# the environment the reports are filed under (staging, production); empty takes ENVIRONMENT.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
# Where the API and the worker serve their metrics in Prometheus's format, as host:port, for a
# scraper on the deployment's own network: requests by route, database connections, the models'
# requests, time and tokens, stages, jobs and uploads, and from a worker the documents and the
# queue by status, the answers being written, the organisations and their bytes by plan, and
# the day's spend. No label names an organisation, a user or anything a member typed. Never a
# port the proxy forwards. Empty serves none; 127.0.0.1:9464 serves them on this machine, and the
# image listens on 0.0.0.0:9464 of its container (deploying.md, "Metrics").
# METRICS_ADDRESS=

# The AI model writes the summaries and facts, reads scans and images, and answers in the Ask
# tab. Leave the key empty to run without those: parsing, outlines and search still work.
# The default model is Gemini; create a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Such a key is on Gemini's free tier until billing is on for its Google Cloud project, and on
# the free tier Google may use what it is sent to improve its products and have people read
# it: fine for the sample documents, not for anyone else's. Production refuses google: models
# until GOOGLE_PAID_TIER=true says billing is on (deploying.md, "What leaves the deployment").
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_PAID_TIER=false

# Models, written provider:model. Indexing is the high-volume side; the agent answers in Ask.
# The default is Google's model for new projects; since September 2026 Google serves Gemini 2.5
# only to accounts that used it before. Production refuses to run on the default, and on an
# alias the provider moves to a newer model when it likes (gemini-flash-latest and the like):
# a deployment names the version it was tried with.
# AGENT_MODEL=google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# INDEX_MODEL=google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#
# The model asked instead when the one above cannot answer: its provider is down, overloaded or
# out of reach, or refuses the key, the account or the model, as it does once the model is
# retired. Not for a request it refused or a reply that took too long. Best one of another
# provider, with its own key below; it is sent what the first is, so it is listed as a
# subprocessor, held to DATA_REGION and the training promise, and needs its price in
# MODEL_PRICES. A model for indexing must read images and one for answering must call tools:
# `uv run python -m app.maintenance models` asks every model named here what the app asks of it.
# AGENT_FALLBACK_MODEL=anthropic:claude-haiku-5-5
# INDEX_FALLBACK_MODEL=anthropic:claude-haiku-5-5
#
# To switch, name another model and add the key its provider reads, for example:
#   google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite               GOOGLE_API_KEY
#   google-cloud:gemini-3.5-flash-lite         Vertex AI: GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS (a
#                                              service account's key file), GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT
#                                              and GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION (us-central1, or eu)
#   anthropic:claude-opus-5-5                  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   openrouter:google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite    OPENROUTER_API_KEY (any model OpenRouter lists;
#                                              each request goes only to a provider of the model
#                                              that neither stores nor trains on it)
#   openai:<a model OpenAI lists>              OPENAI_API_KEY, and OPENAI_BASE_URL for another
#                                              address that speaks OpenAI's API
#   ollama:qwen3.5:4b                          OLLAMA_BASE_URL, http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1 for an
#                                              Ollama on this machine
# The two models can come from different providers. Others that Pydantic AI supports follow the
# same pattern: https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/models/overview/ (deploying.md, "Every setting",
# lists every variable the app reads).
#
# How hard the model thinks: minimal | low | medium | high | xhigh. Set it empty to leave the
# model's own default. Models that cannot think ignore it.
# AGENT_EFFORT=medium
# INDEX_EFFORT=low
#
# The rounds of tool calls one answer may make before it is stopped with what it has. Each
# round is a model request, so this bounds what a question can cost.
# AGENT_MAX_TURNS=12
#
# What the models cost, for the cost on every usage row and the figure under each answer and
# document: model=in/out per million tokens, separated by commas, with the models named as
# above or as EMBEDDING_MODEL (below), in PRICE_CURRENCY (an ISO 4217 code). Empty, no cost
# is recorded. An embedding model is priced for the tokens its endpoint reports (out is 0,
# text-embedding-3-small=0.02/0); one on the deployment's own machines costs nothing known.
# MODEL_PRICES=google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite=0.30/2.50
# PRICE_CURRENCY=USD
#
# What every organisation together may spend on models in one day (UTC), in PRICE_CURRENCY, by
# the costs above: past it, every question is refused with 503 until midnight UTC and every
# indexing stage that needs a model waits for it. 0 is no limit. Each plan has a limit of its
# own for one organisation in backend/app/entitlements.py. Both count nothing without
# MODEL_PRICES, nor anything of a model it has no price for, so production refuses this one
# without a price for every model above, fallbacks included. With ALERT_EMAIL set, the worker
# mails a warning once a day when a day's spending reaches 80 % of a limit, the platform's or
# an organisation's. Set a budget at each provider too (Google Cloud billing budgets for the
# Gemini key, the Anthropic console's spend limits, OpenRouter's credit limit): the app counts
# what it knows the price of, the provider counts the bill.
# DAILY_SPEND_CAP=0
#
# The kill switch: the model providers switched off, by the prefix of a provider:model name,
# separated by commas. A model of a provider named here cannot be used, and the app runs as it
# does without a key (no summaries, facts, scans or Ask tab, with the reason shown) until the
# name is removed and the backend and the worker restarted. For a bill that is running away or
# a key that leaked, when midnight is too far off.
# DISABLED_PROVIDERS=google,anthropic

# Embeddings for semantic search: any OpenAI-compatible /embeddings endpoint.
# The default is a local Ollama (run `ollama pull nomic-embed-text` once). Production refuses
# to start without one named (deploy/compose.yml runs llama.cpp's server with the same model as
# http://embeddings:8080/v1) or with one that turns the start's request down (deploying.md,
# "The embeddings endpoint").
# Changing the model re-embeds every document on the next start. Changing the size needs
# `uv run python -m app.maintenance rebuild-embeddings` (in backend/, app stopped): the app
# refuses to start until the vector column has been resized, and says so. An endpoint outside
# the deployment's own network is sent every passage, so production takes it over https:// only.
# A hosted one's tokens are priced by MODEL_PRICES under EMBEDDING_MODEL's name.
# EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# EMBEDDING_DIM=768
# EMBEDDING_QUERY_PREFIX="search_query: "
# EMBEDDING_DOCUMENT_PREFIX="search_document: "

# Database. The app has no default: a process without DATABASE_URL stops and says so. This
# is the container docker-compose.yml runs, published on 127.0.0.1 only, with the password
# that file sets; a deployment runs its own database and sets DATABASE_URL in the
# environment. POSTGRES_PORT is the host port the container is published on; change the
# two together. A database outside the deployment's own network, such as a managed one, is
# reached with its certificate checked, which production requires: add
# ?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=system to the URL (or the path of the provider's CA file in
# place of system). With `system`, the driver finds the machine's authorities only through
# SSL_CERT_FILE in the environment, such as /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt, and production
# refuses to start without it; the image sets it (deploying.md, "Encryption"). The database
# needs pgvector 0.8 or later, and its role a statement_timeout and an
# idle_in_transaction_session_timeout, which production warns without (deploying.md, "The
# database"); `python -m app.maintenance database` reports on both and on vacuum.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://emudoc:emudoc@127.0.0.1:5437/emudoc
# POSTGRES_PORT=5437
# A pooler between the app and the database: `transaction` when DATABASE_URL goes through one in
# transaction mode (pgbouncer's usual setup), which the app then sends no prepared statement.
# Empty for a direct connection or a pooler in session mode, which need nothing.
# DATABASE_POOLER=
# Where the worker listens for queued jobs when DATABASE_URL goes through a pooler in
# transaction mode, which cannot carry a LISTEN: the database itself, or the pooler's port in
# session mode. Empty, the worker listens on DATABASE_URL, or behind such a pooler polls every
# two seconds and says so. `docker compose --profile pgbouncer up -d --wait` runs a pgbouncer
# in front of the development database to try them (docker-compose.yml has the three lines).
# DATABASE_LISTEN_URL=

# Text search configuration written into each passage as it is indexed. Changing it affects
# documents indexed afterwards; re-run the structure stage to re-index older ones.
# FTS_LANGUAGE=english
# The worker's threads: this many documents indexed at once, and this many questions answered at
# once, in lanes of their own so that a backlog of uploads never holds an answer up. Both spend
# most of their time waiting on the model, so a thread costs a database connection more than
# memory or CPU; raise them together with the pool below. Of the indexers, PARSE_CONCURRENCY
# parse a file at once, each in a child process that may hold PARSE_MEMORY_MB (below), and the
# others wait their turn: the worker's memory bounds the parses, not the indexers.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=16
# ANSWER_CONCURRENCY=8
# PARSE_CONCURRENCY=2
# Whether this process runs the background worker (indexing, housekeeping) as well. Unset, it
# does in development, so that one `uvicorn` does everything, and does not in production, where
# the deployment runs `uv run python -m app.worker` (in backend/) as its own process and scales
# it apart from the API; without any worker, uploads stay queued. true runs both in one process
# on a small deployment; false in development tries the worker as its own process.
# RUN_WORKER=
# The database connections this process keeps: DB_POOL_SIZE open at all times, and up to
# DB_MAX_OVERFLOW more while those are all in use. A connection is held from a session's first
# statement to its commit, so the API needs about one per request in flight, and a worker one
# per document and per answer it runs at once (an answer hands its back while the model thinks)
# plus four for its heartbeat, housekeeper, alerter and metrics; a process that runs the worker
# refuses to start with a pool that cannot hold them (WORKER_CONCURRENCY + ANSWER_CONCURRENCY +
# 4, 28 by default). The worker's listener holds one more connection outside the pool. Keep the
# sum over every replica under the database's max_connections (Postgres allows 100). When every
# connection is in use, a request waits DB_POOL_TIMEOUT seconds for one and is then refused with
# 503 and asked to try again, rather than hanging.
# DB_POOL_SIZE=10
# DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=20
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT=5
# The questions one organisation may have waiting for or receiving an answer at once. The worker
# answers ANSWER_CONCURRENCY of them at a time, shared between the organisations, so the cap
# bounds how long a question waits without letting one organisation keep the others waiting;
# the question past it is refused with 429 and asked to wait a moment. It is the same for every
# organisation; the caps that differ by plan (projects, documents, pages and questions a month)
# are the organisation's entitlements in backend/app/entitlements.py, set by its plan.
# MAX_ANSWERS=20
# The longest question, in characters. A question goes to the model whole with every turn of
# its answer, so one that is pasted rather than asked is refused with 413 and asked to be
# shortened; the documents are what to upload.
# MAX_QUESTION_CHARS=20000
# What one upload may bring: the size of a file, how many files one request carries and their
# bytes together. A file over its size is rejected on its own while the others go in; a
# request over the other two is refused whole with 413 and a sentence that says to upload in
# smaller batches. The body has been read by then, so a proxy's limit on the request body is
# the guard against the request itself; these bound what the app stores from one.
# MAX_UPLOAD_MB=100
# MAX_UPLOAD_FILES=100
# MAX_UPLOAD_REQUEST_MB=500
# How fast one caller may go, in requests a minute: upload requests, questions and searches
# (the search and facts routes) per user; and the sign-in routes (sign-in, sign-up, the reset
# and verification mails, the authenticator code), which have no user yet, per email address
# named and, with more room since an office shares one, per client address. The request past
# a rate is refused with 429 and the seconds until the minute turns. The counts live in the
# database, so every API replica counts the same. 0 removes a limit. A browser that signed in
# to the account before is counted by itself, not under the address, so that a stranger who
# knows the address cannot use up its owner's rate.
# RATE_UPLOADS=20
# RATE_QUESTIONS=20
# RATE_SEARCHES=120
# RATE_SIGN_INS=10
# RATE_SIGN_INS_PER_ADDRESS=60
# The wrong passwords and authenticator codes an account may be tried with in an hour: from the
# browsers that never signed in to it, together, and from each one that has, by itself; and
# from a signed-in session confirming a change, per user. Past it those sign-ins are paused
# until the hour ends, the right password included, and the owner is told by mail of a pause
# of the new browsers; a new password ends it. 0 removes it.
# RATE_FAILED_SIGN_INS=20
# The page's reports of the errors it rendered into, per client address.
# RATE_PAGE_ERRORS=10
# Where uploaded files and rendered page images are kept: a directory on this machine, or an
# S3-compatible bucket with an optional prefix. The default is backend/data inside the checkout,
# for development; a deployment must name a place of its own. A bucket is reached with the
# standard AWS variables, which the S3 library reads from the environment (in development they
# are copied from this file like the model keys): AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION (the bucket's region; URLs are signed for it) and, for MinIO, Cloudflare
# R2, Backblaze B2 or another compatible service, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL. The bucket stays private:
# files are served through the API, which redirects to URLs signed for 15 minutes. At rest the
# bucket's own default encryption applies; the backend asks the bucket which at start and logs
# it (with s3:GetEncryptionConfiguration), and warns when it has none. A directory is as
# encrypted as its disk. Production takes an AWS_ENDPOINT_URL outside the deployment's own
# network over https:// only.
# `docker compose --profile s3 up -d --wait` runs a local bucket to try this with (see
# docker-compose.yml for its settings).
# STORAGE_URL=file:///var/lib/emudoc
# STORAGE_URL=s3://my-bucket/emudoc
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=eu-central-1
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=
# How long a rendered page image or thumbnail is kept after it was rendered, in days. They are
# rendered from the original the first time they are asked for; every few hours the backend
# removes the ones older than this (`uv run python -m app.maintenance sweep-page-images` in
# backend/ does it at once), and a page asked for again is rendered again. 0 keeps them for good.
# PAGE_IMAGE_DAYS=30
# Limits on parsing an upload. The parse stage runs the parser in a child process of the worker,
# so that a file that takes too long or too much memory stops that process alone: the seconds
# a parse may take, the memory (address space) it may hold, the most pages a PDF and rows one
# sheet may have, and the most characters of text one document may hold, which every later
# stage pays for (its passages, about one per 1,200 to 2,000 characters, are written, indexed
# and embedded). A file over a limit fails with a message that names the limit, and is not
# retried. 0 removes a limit. A worker runs PARSE_CONCURRENCY parses at once, each with its
# own memory on top of the worker's.
# PARSE_TIMEOUT=300
# PARSE_MEMORY_MB=2048
# PARSE_MAX_PAGES=500
# PARSE_MAX_ROWS=100000
# PARSE_MAX_CHARS=20000000
# Drawing a PDF's pages as images, for the document pane, the thumbnails and OCR. The API and
# the worker each draw in child processes of their own, one page at a time in each: how many
# pages a process draws at once (each child holds about 50 MB while it waits for the next), the
# seconds a page may take and the memory (address space) a child may hold; a page past a limit
# is not drawn and says why. 0 removes a limit. And how many of a PDF's first pages the worker
# draws once the PDF is parsed, so that opening it shows them at once; the others are drawn
# when first looked at. 0 draws none ahead. From a bucket a page is drawn from a copy of the
# original in the temporary directory, which the API keeps five minutes for the next pages, up
# to RENDER_CACHE_MB of copies; 0 keeps none.
# RENDER_PROCESSES=2
# RENDER_TIMEOUT=30
# RENDER_MEMORY_MB=1024
# RENDER_CACHE_MB=256
# PRERENDER_PAGES=3
# The pages of one document the model may read as scans (pages with no text layer, and
# images). Each costs a model call, so a scanned book would cost hundreds; a document over
# this fails with a message that says to split it, before a page is read. 0 removes the limit.
# OCR_MAX_PAGES=100
# The pages of one scan read at once, each drawn and then sent to the model, the next started
# as one is answered: at a hosted model's seconds a page, one at a time made a 300-page scan
# take a quarter of an hour. Each indexer reading a scan sends this many requests at once, so
# a worker of WORKER_CONCURRENCY indexers may send that many times more; lower it if the
# provider's rate limit is met (deploying.md, "Large documents").
# OCR_CONCURRENCY=4
# The requests to the model one document's summaries and facts may take, one for each 40,000
# characters (a long table is shown by its first and last rows) and one to roll them up. A
# longer document is summarised from its start as far as they reach and says so; the rest is
# searched all the same. The day's spending limits are checked before the stage, so this is
# the most one document spends past them. 0 removes the limit.
# ENRICH_MAX_REQUESTS=50
# The ClamAV daemon that scans every upload for malware before it is indexed or served to
# the other members of its project: clamd://host:3310, or clamd:///run/clamav/clamd.ctl for
# one on this machine. The file is streamed to it, so it needs no access to the storage. A
# flagged file is quarantined: the document says so, nobody can open or download it, and the
# worker reports it to ALERT_EMAIL; a daemon that is down holds uploads in the queue until it
# answers. Empty, nothing is scanned and every document says so; `off` is the same choice made
# on purpose, the only form production accepts besides a daemon. clamd's protocol has no
# encryption, so production takes a daemon on the deployment's own network only (a socket, a
# private address, a compose service). `docker compose --profile
# clamav up -d --wait` runs one to try this with (see docker-compose.yml for its limits, which
# must allow a file of MAX_UPLOAD_MB).
# SCANNER_URL=clamd://127.0.0.1:3310

# Who runs the deployment's servers and database, as the subprocessor list at /subprocessors
# names it first; the list's other entries (the models, the bucket, the mail relay, the sign-in
# providers) are read from the settings in this file. Production refuses to start without it.
# HOSTING_PROVIDER="Hetzner Online GmbH, Germany"

# Who runs the deployment, as its terms of service and privacy policy at /terms and /privacy
# name them: the company or person with their postal address, and the address people write to
# about their account, their data and the terms. Production refuses to start without either.
# OPERATOR_NAME="Emudoc GmbH, Example Street 1, 10115 Berlin, Germany"
# OPERATOR_EMAIL=privacy@example.com

# How many days the deployment keeps the backups of its database and bucket, 0 for none. The
# app makes no backups itself; the privacy policy names this as how long a deleted account can
# outlive its deletion. Production refuses to start without it, and with a versioned bucket
# whose lifecycle rule keeps earlier versions longer (deploying.md, "Backups").
# BACKUP_DAYS=35

# How many days the deployment's infrastructure keeps its records of connections: the
# network's flow logs, and the access logs of the bucket members' browsers fetch page images
# and downloads from, which hold their IP addresses and, for a download, the file's name. The
# privacy policy names them and this period when it is set; unset says there are none. The AWS
# module writes it and expires the logs by it (deploying.md, "The account's records").
# ACCESS_LOG_DAYS=30

# How many days the deployment keeps its server logs: what the API, the worker and the
# migrations write to standard error (each request's client address among it), the reverse
# proxy's access log and the database server's log. The privacy policy names this period. The
# app cannot delete what collects its log, so have that delete lines older than this (journald's
# MaxRetentionSec, a collector's retention); production refuses to start without it. The AWS
# module writes it and the instance's journal and RDS keep to it (deploying.md, "Logs").
# SERVER_LOG_DAYS=30

# The region the deployment keeps its documents in, and everything read from them: eu (the
# European Union) or us (the United States); empty promises none. Set, OpenRouter is reached at
# the region's own address and Claude is told to run in the US under us; at start the app checks
# that the models (Vertex AI's GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION, a Bedrock profile or AWS_DEFAULT_REGION,
# OpenAI's European address), the bucket and the embeddings endpoint are in the region, and in
# production refuses to start when one is not or cannot be told (the Gemini Developer API never
# can). The servers and the database are your word: setting it says they are in the region.
# Each organisation records it as it is created. See deploying.md, "Keeping the data in one
# region".
# DATA_REGION=eu

# Accounts and sign-in. Requests carry a session cookie; signing up makes a user and an
# organisation of their own. For development on one machine, LOCAL_ACCOUNT=true lets a request
# without a session act as a local owner account, so no sign-in is needed; never set it where
# the app is reachable by others.
# LOCAL_ACCOUNT=false
# Where the app is reached, for the links in emails and the OAuth redirect URI. Empty takes the
# origin of the request, which is right in development.
# APP_URL=https://emudoc.example.com
# SESSION_DAYS=30
# Where a new password is checked against the passwords exposed in data breaches: a service
# with Have I Been Pwned's range API, sent the first five characters of the password's SHA-1
# alone (a company on the subprocessor list), or a copy of its list on this deployment's own
# network. Empty checks nothing. A service that does not answer lets the password through, and
# the log says so.
# PWNED_PASSWORDS_URL=https://api.pwnedpasswords.com
# The keys the database's secrets are encrypted with: the authenticators' secrets of two-factor
# sign-in, so that a copy of the database or its backups cannot make anyone's codes. Each is 32
# random bytes in base64, as `openssl rand -base64 32` prints one, separated by commas: the
# first encrypts, every one decrypts what it encrypted. Rotate by putting a new key first, then
# `python -m app.maintenance encrypt-secrets`, and keep the old one while a backup may hold what
# it encrypted (deploying.md, "The encryption keys"). Keep them apart from the database's
# backups. Empty is development's own key, which is in the code; production refuses it.
# ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
#
# Verification and password-reset mail. Empty writes the messages to the backend log, where the
# links can be followed in development. Otherwise an SMTP relay: smtp:// uses STARTTLS (587),
# smtps:// uses TLS (465), and either way the relay's certificate must be one this machine
# trusts, for the relay's own name, or nothing is sent.
# SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587
# MAIL_FROM="Emudoc <no-reply@example.com>"
#
# Where the worker reports the jobs it gave up on: a document lost to a dying worker three
# times, a crash in the pipeline, or a stage that kept failing through every retry. With an
# address set, the worker mails the new failures there every quarter of an hour as one digest,
# reports the uploads the malware scan quarantined the same way, warns there once a day
# when a day's spending on models nears a limit (DAILY_SPEND_CAP above, or a plan's), and
# writes there when its watch sees something go wrong and when it is over: a backlog, indexing
# or answers that fail, a model failing over, the embeddings endpoint, the scanner or the
# storage not answering, the database falling behind (deploying.md, "Alerts"). Empty sends
# nothing; `uv run python -m app.maintenance failed-jobs` (in backend/) lists the failures
# either way, and runs them again with --requeue.
# ALERT_EMAIL=
#
# Sign in with Google or Microsoft. Register an OAuth client with each provider, with the
# redirect URI {APP_URL}/api/auth/oauth/google/callback (or .../microsoft/callback), and put
# its id and secret here. A provider without an id is not offered.
# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (a Web application client).
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Microsoft: an app registration in Entra ID with a web redirect URI. "common" accepts work,
# school and personal accounts; a tenant id restricts sign-in to that directory. Add the
# optional claim xms_edov to the ID token so that verified addresses are recognised as such.
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_TENANT=common
